Hi Karen,
Yes, I believe that is correct.  The Mahalanobis distance is only defined relatively, for pairs of 2 subjects (i.e., the distance between A and B is different from the distance between A and C).  It is not like propensity score matching, where each person has a particular value and then we match on that-with Mahalanobis matching person "A" doesn't have their own value; the matches are defined only by calculating their distance with the other individuals in the data.  So there isn't really a simple "distance" variable/measure on which you could look at the diagnostics.

I hope this helps.

Liz


